//
// Phase C4: every baseline_768 shape resolves through the builder that owns it.
//
// The sub-phase's whole point is that the 36 projection GEMMs the case matrix's
// baseline_768 family needs stop raising. This is the regression test for that,
// and it is deliberately the cheap half: it re-reads the rows the sweep wrote
// and resolves each one. It does not re-measure anything. The sweep that
// produced the rows takes an hour on real hardware and is `make registry-sweep`,
// not a lit test.
//
// Resolution is checked THROUGH THE PRODUCTION BUILDERS -- `qkv_gemm_spec`,
// `ffn_gemm_specs`, `o_proj_gemm_spec` -- not through `registry_lookup` in the
// abstract, and the difference is not cosmetic. `gemm_config()` answers "what is
// this row's fastest high-precision method". `build_qkv_proj_module` cannot use
// that answer: its three-way C split rides `fused-cast`'s separate cast launch,
// so it asks for `fused-cast` by name, and `drain` is the recorded winner at
// most of the ladder. `64x768x2304` was registered with `drain` and `direct`
// only; it satisfied a generic-lookup check while the operator that needs it
// raised. Hence the second command below, which pins every QKV point to the
// method its builder can actually emit.
//
// npu2 in the filename is the TARGET THE ROWS DESCRIBE -- kernel_registry is an
// NPU2-only registry (`"target": "npu2"` in the JSON) and every tile config in
// it was measured on AIE2P. The check itself dispatches nothing and needs
// neither a device nor Peano, which is exactly why it is cheap enough to gate
// on. Adding a hardware requirement here would only stop the regression from
// surfacing on a compile-only runner.
//
// The count is part of the gate. A registry write that dropped half the family
// would still let the surviving shapes resolve, and a check that only looked for
// a final PASS would report success on it -- so the number of resolutions is
// pinned, and nothing may resolve after the last one.
//
// So is the METHOD. `gemm_builder.gemm_method_spec` knows only `fused-cast` and
// `drain`; a row whose high-precision winner was recorded as `direct` would
// resolve here perfectly happily and then raise `unknown gemm method` inside
// every model that read it. Requiring one of the two buildable names turns that
// into a failure here instead of a failure three phases downstream. The QKV
// pass narrows that further to `fused-cast` alone, on all nine points, because
// for that operator any other method is a build failure and not a slow row.
//
// So is the BUILDER NAME in each line. It is what shows the resolution came
// from the operator rather than from the generic lookup standing in for it, so
// a future edit that quietly reverts to `gemm_config()` cannot keep this
// passing.
//
// So is the HERD. The ladder starts at 64, which cannot hold 8 rows of either
// method's forced tile_m, so those rows carry a herd of their own and the
// lookup has to hand it back -- a builder that assumed the file-level 8x4 there
// trips the example's own divisibility assert before it compiles anything.
// Pinning the herd in the resolved line is what keeps that value wired through
// the lookup rather than dropped on the floor as an unread JSON key.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// RUN: python3 %S/registry_sweep.py --family baseline_768 --verify-resolution | FileCheck %s
// RUN: python3 %S/registry_sweep.py --family baseline_768 --role qkv_proj --verify-resolution | FileCheck --check-prefix=QKV %s
//
// CHECK-COUNT-36: [resolve] {{[0-9]+x[0-9]+x[0-9]+}} ({{.*}}) -> {{build_[a-z_]+_module}} {{fused-cast|drain}} {{[0-9]+/[0-9]+/[0-9]+/[0-9]+}} herd {{[0-9]+x[0-9]+}}
// CHECK-NOT: [resolve]
// CHECK: PASS: all 36 baseline_768 shapes resolve
//
// QKV-COUNT-9: [resolve] {{[0-9]+x768x2304}} (qkv_proj, {{.*}}) -> build_qkv_proj_module fused-cast {{64/[0-9]+/[0-9]+/[0-9]+}} herd {{[0-9]+x[0-9]+}}
// QKV-NOT: [resolve]
// QKV: PASS: all 9 baseline_768 shapes resolve
